security | No checks apply from code; runbook, alert routing, fleet impact query and outside-reporting owner are not in this repo. | Run the E12 drill and keep the impact query, quarantine and revocation records, alert times and the named reporting decision-maker. Also note gateway stop query (gateway.py:99) ignores agent_type scope (see R11). | [] [] False False\n    1500\nagent.py\napprovals.py\naudit.py\nbudgets.py\ncredentials.py\ngateway_client.py\ngateway.py\nkillswitch.py\nmain.py\nredact.py\nretrieval.py\ntools.py\ntype_id.py\nvalidation.py","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a775c57f-3431-4870-9216-f1a3261464e8","uuid":"44ff5c1c-0b24-457f-b2e8-9e5a5a4b803c","timestamp":"2026-11-21T12:30:39.065Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"dict_keys(['brace_checklist', 'repo', 'commit', 'reviewed_at', 'review_protocol', 'scope', 'findings', 'injection_attempts', 'prescan_resolutions'])\nB01 gap G1 agent_team | The agent has a fixed four-tool allowlist, but escalate_to_human can fall back to calling the helpdesk MCP server directly, skipping the gateway allowlist and disabled-tool check. | Remove direct_route from escalate_to_human in config/tools.yaml and the fallback in src/gateway_client.py so gateway outages fail closed. | [('config/tools.yaml', 17)] [\"Enforce a role-based allowlist, and check that an unlisted tool can't be called\", 'List every built-in tool, MCP server, API, program, and file permission this agent type can use. Give a task-based reason for each. Enforce a role-based allowlist, and check that an unlisted tool can\\'t be called. Don\\'t base allow or approval decisions on hints a server gives about its own tools, such as \"read-only.\" MCP treats those hints as untrusted unless you trust the server.'] False False\nB02 gap G1 infrastructure | A cache in retrieval.py keys on query text only, so one employee's employee-only handbook chunks can be served to another employee's search. | Add the employee id to the cache key in src/retrieval.py _key(), or drop caching for employee-audience chunks; then collect IAM and STS evidence for B02.1, B02.3 and B02.4. | [('src/retrieval.py', 42)] ['including tenant boundaries', \"Limit credentials to named operations and resources, including tenant boundaries. Identify the acting agent separately from the original user or system whose authority it uses. Explicitly authorized, narrowly scoped user delegation is valid; copying a login session or inheriting a person's full permissions is not. Test that an allowed read works and an out-of-scope write, an admin action, and another tenant's data all fail. Limit each access token to its intended audience, so other services reject it. Remove wildcard grants.\"] False False\nB03 gap G2 infrastructure | Runtime token exchange is short-lived and certificate-bound at the gateway, but rotation of the static DB and Anthropic secrets and downstream token binding are set outside this repo. | Collect the secret rotation policy for hr-agent-db, hr-gateway-db and hr-agent-anthropic, the DB auth options, and STS proof that downstream tokens carry cnf binding. | [('k8s/deployment.yaml', 54)] ['static API keys', 'record the lifetime and revocation gap', 'Show expiry and rotation', \"Issue short-lived credentials from a trusted issuer or managed identity service. For API calls, use expiring access tokens with a specific destination audience, identifying the agent and any explicitly delegated user or system authority, rather than static API keys or personal access tokens. A secrets vault stores backing secrets; it does not itself make a static key into an expiring access token. Keep long-lived secrets out of prompts, images, and checked-in config. Where supported, bind tokens to their holder (mTLS, DPoP, or workload proof tokens). Don't use fixed API keys when short-lived or federated credentials are available. If only static keys are supported, isolate them at the enforcement layer, limit their permissions, and record the lifetime and revocation gap. Show expiry and rotation, including allowed overlap, and explain the chosen lifetime.\"] False False\nB04 gap G1 infrastructure | Stop flags cut off the agent identity at the gateway without affecting the employee, but the direct-route fallback and agent_type flags bypass that enforcement point while tokens last 300 seconds. | Remove direct_route fallback for escalate_to_human, add agent_type to the gateway stop query, and document the revocation limit against the 300 second token TTL. | [('src/gateway.py', 101)] ['A trusted enforcement point must block later calls within a time limit', \"Show that an operator can cut off this agent's access without cutting off the user who launched it. The agent must not need to cooperate. Set and measure the longest time before the cutoff takes effect. Count cached tokens, self-contained tokens, and open sessions. A trusted enforcement point must block later calls within a time limit that fits the potential harm. Revoking only the refresh token is not enough. If tokens live longer than that limit, push the revocation to each service (for example, with OpenID CAEP session-revoked events) or make the tokens shorter-lived.\"] False False\nB05 gap G1 agent_team | The approval gate covers all consequential tools, but the leave-request approval text omits the model-written note, so the employee approves arguments they were not shown. | Include the note in describe() for hris/file_leave_request in src/approvals.py, or remove the note field from the input schema. | [('config/tools.yaml', 61)] ['approves that specific action'] False False\nB06 gap G1 agent_team | Approvals are bound, single-use and atomic, but the reviewer is not shown the note field that is sent with a leave request. | Include every approved argument, including note, in describe() in src/approvals.py and add a test that the description contains it. | [('src/approvals.py', 80)] ['Show the reviewer what the action will do'] False False\nB07 gap G2 ag